<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#black-ships">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Black Ships</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1853">1853</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Black Ships was the Japanese name for the Western vessels, especially the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steamship">steam-powered warships</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthew-c-perry">Commodore Matthew Perry</a>'s American squadron that entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-bay">Edo Bay</a> in 1853. Perry demanded the opening of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> to American commerce. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>'s inability to resist or accommodate the demand exposed the military weakness of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/black-ships.jpg" alt="Black Ships" width="320" height="206" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_1854_print_Commodore_Perry.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The term kurofune first named Portuguese ships. Portuguese traders reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1543 and opened a route from their headquarters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a>, by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a>. Their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrack">carracks</a> of 1,200 to 1,600 tons, called nau do trato or nau da <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, had hulls painted black with pitch, and the name came to cover all Western vessels; it was recorded in the Nippo Jisho, the first Western dictionary of Japanese, compiled in 1603. The Nanban trade brought refined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, optics and firearms, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arquebus">arquebus</a>, copied in Japan, became a major weapon of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-xavier">Francis Xavier</a> began a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> mission in 1549, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> won some 300,000 converts. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimabara-rebellion">Shimabara Rebellion</a>, blamed on Christian influence, was suppressed in 1637, the Portuguese were expelled in 1639, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> kept to the policy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakoku">Sakoku</a>, the locked country, trading only with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryukyu-islands">Ryukyu Islands</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dejima">Dejima</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The name passed to the four American warships of Commodore Matthew C. Perry's expedition, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, Plymouth, Saratoga and Susquehanna, which anchored at Uraga in July 1853. Their hulls were not black, but their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>-fired engines poured out black smoke, and ships that moved without wind deeply frightened onlookers. Perry refused requests to go to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a>, the licensed port, and threatened to attack Edo and burn it if he could not land; he was allowed ashore at Kurihama, delivered his letter and left. He returned the next year with eight ships, and after about a month of talks the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/convention-of-kanagawa">Convention of Kanagawa</a> opened formal relations. Townsend Harris stayed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimoda">Shimoda</a> as consul, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harris-treaty">Harris Treaty</a> followed on 29 July 1858.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The kurofune became a symbol of the end of isolation. A kyōka of the time plays on pivot words: read one way it tells of a costly green <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a>, Jōkisen, four cups of which keep a drinker awake; read another it tells of four steamships, jōkisen, that break the slumber of the Pacific and leave the country sleepless. Kurofune is also the title of the first Japanese opera, by Kosaku Yamada, first performed in 1940 and based on the story of the geisha Tojin Okichi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Black Ships” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The shogunate's military weakness was exposed in 1853 when Commodore Matthew Perry's '<b>Black Ships</b>', a squadron that included steam-powered warships armed with modern guns, entered Edo Bay and demanded that Japan open its ports to American ships.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Military</a></li></ul></section>
